I started on funservers in early TBC, something in the ArcEmu line, though I
could not tell you which fork at this distance. Then years of 3.3.5 on
TrinityCore. Vanilla MaNGOS is the one I actually enjoy working with, and that
scene only really got going around 2017. I never properly stopped. I just left
off hosting anything public after a few cease and desist letters arrived.

So it is the workload I know well enough to test a cluster honestly. Split one
up and almost all of it is boring, stateless plumbing that belongs in
[the k3s platform](/blog/k3s-platform-kit/). One process does not, and that one
is the game.

That split is the useful part, and not just for game servers. Most systems have
one component that quietly cannot be scheduled, and finding it before you build
the platform is cheaper than finding it after.

These projects exist to study game server architecture and network protocol
design, and that is the only use I am discussing. If you run anything, the
legality of it is your own responsibility.

::: note
**Part 2 of 2 of [Where to start with
self-hosting](/blog/running-things-on-your-own-hardware/).** Previous: [K3s
starter kit on Hetzner cloud](/blog/k3s-platform-kit/)
:::

## The three pieces

An authentication daemon that answers the login handshake, a world daemon that
is the game, and a relational database split into auth, characters and world
content. The same three in TrinityCore, AzerothCore, CMaNGOS, MaNGOS and AscEmu.

The database is where the time actually went. The servers I ran broke on data
far more often than on code: a world database half-migrated by whoever was
developing against it, DBC files mangled on extraction, an MPQ archive missing
so the maps came out wrong. None of that is an architecture problem, which is
worth saying before a post that treats the architecture as the interesting part.

The first two are joined by a database table rather than an API. TrinityCore's
auth database has a `realmlist` row per realm with `address` and `port`
([realmlist](https://github.com/TrinityCore/tc-wiki/blob/main/database/335/auth/realmlist.md)),
documented as the world server's public address. The client logs in, gets that
address, and opens a raw TCP connection straight to the world daemon.

I had expected it to talk to a front door that routes. It does not, and that
settles the topology before you get a say in it. Whatever sits in front of the
world daemon has to be a transparent TCP path. An ingress that rewrites
hostnames has nothing to rewrite.

## Why the world server must have a machine of its own

TrinityCore's map manager walks every live map once per tick and either updates
them directly or hands them to a pool of workers, then waits for all of them:

{title="src/server/game/Maps/MapManager.cpp"}
```cpp
if (m_updater.activated())
    m_updater.schedule_update(*iter->second, uint32(i_timer.GetCurrent()));
else
    iter->second->Update(uint32(i_timer.GetCurrent()));
...
if (m_updater.activated())
    m_updater.wait();
```

And the unit of work a worker gets is one whole map. So parallelism exists
between maps and instances but never inside one: a continent is a single `Map`
object, and updating it is a single task on a single thread. `m_updater.wait()`
is also a barrier on every tick, which means the tick costs whatever the slowest
individual map costs, however many threads are idle behind it.

The shipped defaults agree. TrinityCore ships `MapUpdate.Threads = 1`, CMaNGOS
ships 3, and both ship `Network.Threads = 1` with the note "Recommended 1 thread
per 1000 connections". All of that comes from the source and the config files
rather than from profiling a populated server, so take it as the design and not
as a measurement. People do report a crowded capital city pinning one core,
which fits.

I went in assuming a modern core with a thread pool would spread across cores,
and that a busy realm just wanted a bigger machine. What it wants is clock
speed, and the fastest single thread you can buy tends to sit in a desktop
socket. PassMark gives the Ryzen 9 9950X 4,728 on single thread; the 192-core
EPYC 9965 gets 3,176.

I met this on cheap OVH VPS instances years before I could have told you why.
The symptom matched the code above: fine most of the time, then not fine, with
cores sitting there that nothing could spend. I never profiled it, so that is a
matching symptom and not a diagnosis.

Now put that process in a cluster, and the Kubernetes documentation describes
what you just agreed to:

> By default, the kubelet uses CFS quota to enforce pod CPU limits. When the
> node runs many CPU-bound pods, the workload can move to different CPU cores
> depending on whether the pod is throttled and which CPU cores are available
> at scheduling time.

That is from [CPU Management
Policies](https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/cpu-management-policies/),
describing normal, correct behaviour. Getting exclusive cores back takes the
`static` policy, and even then only for containers that are "both part of a
`Guaranteed` pod and have integer CPU `requests`". The fix is kubelet
configuration at the machine level, and what you end up with is a machine
dedicated to one pod, with pinned cores and reserved memory, still sitting under
a control plane that believes it can evict you.

Three more things do not fit.

- The state lives in RAM and it keeps growing. AzerothCore's memory page says
  the server caches maps as players explore them and never frees them until a
  restart, ending up at 11 GB or more, and suggests starting at 16 GB. So
  restarting a pod restarts the server for everyone logged in, and the cache
  then has to warm up again from nothing.
- Readiness does not mean much here. A world server that has finished loading is
  not interchangeable with the one it replaced.
- The connection is a long-lived raw TCP session. Ingress controllers, retries
  and connection draining all assume requests that end.

## What belongs in the cluster

Everything else.

- The authentication daemon. Its durable state is in the database, so the
  process itself is close to stateless.
- The website, the registration API, the account panel: ordinary CRUD over that
  same database.
- Item shops, armory pages, ladders, statistics. Read-mostly and cacheable.
- Discord bots, status pages, uptime checks.
- Backups, schema migrations and content-database imports, which is what Jobs
  and CronJobs are for.
- Observability for all of the above, and for the world machine too, which can
  push into the cluster without living in it.

That is an ordinary small platform, and it is the shape the
[k3s kit](/blog/k3s-platform-kit/) generates: GitOps delivery, a namespace per
tenant, certificates, secrets, observability. So the architecture that fits is a
cluster for all of that and one private machine for the world, with the cluster
proxying through to it at layer 4.

## Where the line falls

Split the system by whether a process can be killed and replaced without anyone
noticing. Auth, web, shop, armory, statistics, bots, backups and jobs all pass
that test, and a cluster gives them reconciled configuration, generated
isolation and one place to look when something breaks.

The world server fails it. One long-lived process holding a world in memory,
advancing it on a clock, bound by a single thread, talking raw TCP to clients
who will notice a restart within a second. Fast core, plenty of RAM, local disk,
short path to the players. That is [one machine](/blog/athletos-freebsd/).

None of which is an argument against Kubernetes. It is an argument against
assuming one system has one shape, which is the assumption that puts the world
server in a pod in the first place.
